<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	The signed-in account's own settings for the assistant (Issue #437).

	One field: the free text the user writes about how they want to be served - how to address them,
	how long an answer should be, which language to prefer when several would do. It is an attribute
	of tl.accounts:Person, added by tl-ai-core, so every agent the user talks to reads the same text,
	in the chat and in a scheduled run alike. The harness renders it into the environment block of
	the system prompt, below what the application itself states, and tells the model that the
	application wins where the two disagree.

	The length cap is configuration (AgentPolicyService, max-preferences-length), so it is
	enforced by a constraint on the attribute rather than by this form: a text above it is refused
	here with a message naming the limit, because the text is sent with every single request.

	The form binds to a derived channel of its own rather than to the shell's "currentPerson":
	a view is self-contained, and the same file then works wherever it is referenced.
-->
<view>
	<channels>
		<derived-channel name="account"
			expr="currentUser()"
		/>
	</channels>

	<panel fill="true">
		<title>
			<en>My settings</en>
			<de>Meine Einstellungen</de>
		</title>
		<form input="account" with-inset="true">
			<field attribute="aiPreferences"/>
		</form>
	</panel>
</view>
